Mica adds an editing interface to an Astro project. The website still reads local files and builds to static output.

> Mica is experimental and pre-1.0. Its API and content format can change. Use it for work that you can update as the project changes.

## What you need

- Node 22.5 or newer
- An Astro 5 project
- A Git repository for the project

## 1. Install the packages

```bash
npm install @micacms/astro @micacms/model
```

## 2. Define a content model

Create `src/mica.ts`. The model is a plain TypeScript module and does not import Astro.

```ts
import { collection, defineSite, richText, slug, text } from "@micacms/model";

export const site = defineSite({
  root: "./content",
  collections: {
    news: collection({
      label: "News",
      fields: {
        title: text({ required: true, listed: true }),
        slug: slug({ from: "title" }),
        body: richText(),
      },
    }),
  },
});
```

## 3. Add the Astro collections

Create `src/content.config.ts`.

```ts
import { micaCollections } from "@micacms/astro";
import { site } from "./mica";

export const collections = micaCollections(site);
```

Nothing goes in `astro.config`. Start the Astro dev server once. The loader creates `content/.mica/schema.json`. Commit this file because it tells Mica what your project can edit.

## 4. Start Mica

```bash
npx @micacms/cms
```

Open `http://localhost:4000/admin/`. Local mode uses no account or password and listens only on the local computer.

## 5. Render the content

Use normal Astro Content Layer calls.

```astro
---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
const news = await getCollection("news");
---

{news.map((article) => <h2>{article.data.title}</h2>)}
```

Run `git diff` after an editor saves. You will see the complete content change as JSON, Markdoc, and media files.
